@page "/"

<PageTitle>BlazorSync · Local-first sync for .NET</PageTitle>

<div class="hero">
    <h1>BlazorSync</h1>
    <p class="hero-sub">
        A local-first sync engine for .NET. Your app reads and writes a local store instantly and
        works fully offline; changes replicate to the cloud in the background and conflicts resolve
        with a strategy you control.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn primary" href="playground">Open the Sync Playground →</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="conflicts">Try the Conflict Lab</a>
    </div>
    <p class="hero-note">
        This demo runs entirely in your browser on Blazor WebAssembly. The "cloud" is an in-process
        reference server, so everything you see — local writes, offline edits, checkpoint sync,
        conflict resolution — is the real BlazorSync engine, just without a network hop.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-icon">⚡</div>
        <h3>Instant local writes</h3>
        <p>Reads and writes hit a local store, so the UI never waits on the network. Try editing a note with a device set to offline.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-icon">📴</div>
        <h3>Offline-first</h3>
        <p>Toggle any device offline. It keeps working; queued changes flush automatically on the next sync once it is back online.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-icon">⟲</div>
        <h3>Checkpoint sync</h3>
        <p>Devices catch up by pulling only what changed since their last checkpoint, then push local writes — the same protocol RxDB popularised.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-icon">⚔️</div>
        <h3>Pluggable conflicts</h3>
        <p>Client-wins by default, or switch any device to server-wins, last-write-wins, or a custom field merge — per device, at runtime.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-icon">⏱️</div>
        <h3>Hybrid Logical Clocks</h3>
        <p>Every write is stamped with an HLC for causal, deterministic ordering that survives clock skew. Explore it on the Clock page.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-icon">🗑️</div>
        <h3>Soft deletes</h3>
        <p>Deletions are tombstoned, not erased, so they replicate to every device. The cloud inspector shows retained tombstones.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="howto">
    <h2>A 60-second tour</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Open the <a href="playground">Sync Playground</a>. You start with two devices and an empty cloud.</li>
        <li>Add a note on <strong>Laptop</strong>, then click its <strong>Sync</strong> button. Watch it appear in the cloud inspector.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Sync</strong> on <strong>Phone</strong> — the note replicates down to it.</li>
        <li>Toggle <strong>Phone</strong> offline. Edit the same note on both devices.</li>
        <li>Sync <strong>Laptop</strong>, bring <strong>Phone</strong> back online and sync it. The conflict resolves using Phone's chosen strategy.</li>
        <li>Flip Phone's strategy to <em>Server wins</em> or <em>Field merge</em> and repeat to compare outcomes.</li>
    </ol>
</div>
